  "id": "COMPOUND:242 - DISEASE:1020",
  "verbalization": "Phenytoin, a widely used anticonvulsant, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Epidemic pleurodynia through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that phenytoin may inhibit the activity of FAT tumor suppressor homolog 1 (Drosophila) pseudogene 1, which is encoded by the gene product Serine/threonine-protein kinase EEF2K. This inhibition could disrupt the tumor suppressor function of this pseudogene, leading to uncontrolled cellular proliferation and contributing to the development of Epidemic pleurodynia. Additionally, phenytoin is shown to modulate G protein signaling, which may also play a role in the pathophysiology of the condition, as it is associated with the causation of Cataract, autosomal recessive congenital 4, and Epidemic pleurodynia. The overlapping involvement of phenytoin in multiple pathways—such as tumor suppressor regulation and G protein signaling—suggests a potential role in the pathogenesis of Epidemic pleurodynia, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence is supported by a consistent link between phenytoin and the condition, with multiple independent pathways converging on the same target, enhancing the likelihood of a mechanistic relationship.",
